[ti:William S. Hart and the Early "Western" Film]
[by:更多学习内容，请到VeryCD.com搜索“露珠”]
[00:00.58]Lesson 21 | 第21课
[00:02.87]William S.Hart and the early 'Western' film | 威廉·S·哈特和早期“西部”电影
[00:13.65]How did William Hart's childhood prepare him for his acting role in Western films? | 威廉·哈特的童年是如何为他日后在西部片中扮演的角色做准备的？
[00:22.05]William S.Hart was, perhaps, the greatest of all Western stars, | 威廉·S·哈特也许是一切西部片明星中最伟大的，
[00:29.56]for unlike Gary Cooper and John Wayne he appeared in nothing but Westerns. | 因为与加里·库珀和约翰·韦恩不同，他只在西部片中出现。
[00:36.57]From 1914 to 1924 he was supreme and unchallenged. | 从1914年到1924年，他称雄影坛，无人挑战。
[00:43.99]It was Hart who created the basic formula of the Western film, | 正是哈特创造了西部电影的基本格局，
[00:48.96]and devised the protagonist he played in every film he made, | 并且塑造了他在每部影片中所扮演的主人公形象：
[00:53.95]the good-bad man, the accidental, noble outlaw, | 良心未泯的坏人、意外变成高尚的逃犯，
[00:58.84]or the honest, but framed cowboy, or the sheriff made suspect by vicious gossip; | 或者诚实的却被恶毒流言陷害的牛仔，或者被恶语中伤而遭怀疑的警官；
[01:06.09]in short, the individual in conflict with himself and his frontier environment. | 总之，是一个与自己以及他的边疆环境不断冲突的个人。
[01:13.78]Unlike most of his contemporaries in Hollywood, | 哈特与好莱坞大多数同时代的人不同，
[01:17.06]Hart actually knew something of the old West. | 他确实对昔日的西部有所了解。
[01:21.02]He had lived in it as a child when it was already disappearing, | 他童年时代曾在西部生活过，当时西部正在消失，
[01:25.00]and his hero was firmly rooted in his memories and experiences, | 他塑造的英雄形象深深植根于他的记忆和经历，
[01:30.03]and in both the history and the mythology of the vanished frontier, | 植根于已消失的边疆的历史和神话，
[01:35.67]And although no period or place in American history has been more absurdly romanticized, | 尽管美国历史上没有任何时期或地方曾受到过比这更荒唐的浪漫化渲染，
[01:43.23]myth and reality did join hands in at least one arena, | 神话和现实确实至少在一个舞台上携起手来，
[01:48.63]the conflict between the individual and encroaching civilization. | 那就是个人与步步进逼的文明之间的冲突。
[01:55.22]Men accustomed to struggling for survival against the elements and Indians | 习惯于同大自然和印第安人作生存斗争的人们，
[02:00.82]were bewildered by politicians, bankers and businessmen, | 被政客、银行家和商人弄得晕头转向，
[02:05.36]and unhorsed by fences, laws and alien taboos. | 被围栏、法律和陌生的禁忌摔下了马背。
[02:11.49]Hart's good-bad man was always an outsider, always one of the disinherited, | 哈特塑造的良心未泯的坏人总是局外人，总是被剥夺了继承权的人，
[02:18.91]and if he found it necessary to shoot a sheriff or rob a bank along the way, | 如果他在路途中觉得有必要毙掉一名警官或抢劫一家银行，
[02:25.08]his early audiences found it easy to understand and forgive, | 他的早期观众很容易理解和原谅他，
[02:29.62]especially when it was Hart who, in the end, overcame the attacking Indians. | 尤其当最终制服进攻的印第安人的正是哈特的时候。
[02:36.66]Audiences in the second decade of the twentieth century | 生活在20世纪第二个十年中的观众，
[02:40.32]found it pleasant to escape to a time when life, though hard, was relatively simple. | 发觉逃避到一个虽然艰苦却相对纯朴的时代中去是件愉快的事。
[02:46.64]We still do; living in a world in which undeclared aggression, war, hypocrisy, | 我们至今仍然如此；我们生活在一个未宣而战的侵略、战争、虚伪、
[02:53.66]chicanery, anarchy and impending immolation are part of our daily lives, | 欺诈、无政府状态和临头的毁灭是我们日常生活一部分的世界里，
[02:59.89]we all want a code to live by. | 我们大家都需要有一个可以遵循的行为准则。
